Speed up get_start_time(): read a bounded header chunk, not the whole file.

masscube's workflow preparation (STEP 1) calls get_start_time() once per raw
file, serially, to sort samples by acquisition time. The original implementation
uses f.readlines()[:300], which materializes the ENTIRE file in memory before
slicing off the first 300 lines. For GB-sized mzML files this reads every byte
of every sample just to find one timestamp near the top of the header, with no
progress output — so the workflow appears frozen on
"Step 1: Preparing the workflow...". On a cloud-synced data folder (OneDrive,
Dropbox, network share) it is far worse, because the full read forces every
placeholder file to be downloaded in full.

A bounded 256 KB read is far more than enough for 300 lines of mzML/mzXML
header. Measured on a synthetic 406 MB mzML: 717 ms -> 0.5 ms (~1300x), and
that was a warm local read. Return values are identical for headers with a
timestamp, headers without one, timestamps beyond line 300, and missing files.

Apply against the installed masscube package root, e.g.:

    cd <site-packages>
    patch -p1 < masscube-get-start-time.patch

or run scripts/apply_masscube_patch.py, which does the same edit in place and is
idempotent.

Verified against masscube as installed 2026-05-11 (utils_functions.py, 19345
bytes).

--- a/masscube/utils_functions.py
+++ b/masscube/utils_functions.py
@@ -182,12 +182,17 @@ def get_start_time(file_name):

     if os.path.exists(str(file_name)):
         with open(file_name, "rb") as f:
-            # check the first 300 rows for the start time
-            for l in f.readlines()[:300]:
-                l = str(l)
-                if "startTimeStamp" in str(l):
-                    t = l.split("startTimeStamp")[1].split('"')[1]
-                    return datetime.strptime(t, "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")
+            # Read a bounded header chunk instead of f.readlines(), which pulls the
+            # whole file into memory before the [:300] slice — on GB-sized mzML
+            # files (or cloud-synced folders) that alone can take minutes per file.
+            # 256 KB covers far more than 300 lines of mzML/mzXML header.
+            head = f.read(262144)
+        # check the first 300 rows for the start time
+        for l in head.splitlines()[:300]:
+            l = str(l)
+            if "startTimeStamp" in str(l):
+                t = l.split("startTimeStamp")[1].split('"')[1]
+                return datetime.strptime(t, "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")
     return None
